---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import CaptureThumb from '../../components/CaptureThumb.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import { fig } from '../../lib/figures';

/*
 * Dates and technical transitions checked against release histories, source
 * commits, signed releases and captured primary statements.
 *
 * This page carries both the sparse 2021 to 2026 spine and the dense 30 July to
 * 1 August reconstruction that used to be published separately at
 * /record/disclosure/. Each page spent a paragraph explaining that it was the
 * other one's sibling, and their 30 July to 1 August entries overlapped, in
 * places verbatim. They are one page now: the spine stays plain, and the
 * second-level reconstruction sits in rungs so it does not bury it.
 */
const spine = `<div class="tl">
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-date">28 Jan 2021</div>
    <div class="tl-body">The <code>#ifndef MICROPY_HW_ENABLE_RNG</code> guard exists in <code>libngu</code>. Its defined-zero defect is already present, but COLDCARD seed generation does not yet use this path.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item is-bad">
    <div class="tl-date">1 Mar 2021</div>
    <div class="tl-body">Commit <code>b18723dddb6d751c39978e4364b56b2414f68b47</code> migrates wallet generation from <code>ckcc.rng_bytes()</code> to <code>ngu.random.bytes()</code>. Combined with the existing defined-zero guard defect, the migration activates the affected path.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item is-bad">
    <div class="tl-date">17 Mar 2021</div>
    <div class="tl-body">Firmware v4.0.0 ships for Mk2 and Mk3, opening the first exposure window. Its normal seed-generation path XORs output from the MicroPython and libngu Yasmarang generators before hashing.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-date">11 and 14 Mar 2022</div>
    <div class="tl-body">The <code>reseed()</code> API is added to <code>libngu</code>, and Mk4 v5.0.0 ships as the first production firmware calling <code>rng_seeding()</code> on the normal boot path. A secure-element-derived value overwrites one libngu state word, contributing at most 32 bits; other UID and timing terms remain in published models.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item is-bad">
    <div class="tl-date">26 Jun 2023</div>
    <div class="tl-body">Firmware v4.1.9 ships. For the next three years it remains the latest published Mk3 release, and it contains the affected path.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-date">8 Feb 2024</div>
    <div class="tl-body">First public Q firmware, v0.0.3Q, with production v1.0.0Q on 10 March 2024. Every Q build in the published release history before v1.5.0Q contains the affected path.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-date">10 Mar 2026</div>
    <div class="tl-body">COLDCARD Mk5 launches on firmware v5.5.0 and uses the Mk4-class generation path. Its pre-hotfix releases are affected under the same published candidate-space models.</div>
  </div>
</div>`;
---

<Article
  title="Timeline"
  description="From the guard being written in 2021 to disclosure and model-specific hotfix releases in 2026, with the 30 July to 3 August public record reconstructed in detail."
  standfirst="From the guard being written in 2021 to disclosure and model-specific hotfix releases in 2026. The rungs reconstruct what was public, and when."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="Evidence"
>
  <Answer>
  <p>
    On 30 July 2026, inside a window of about 41 minutes, funds were swept from
    roughly 1,200 bitcoin addresses, and published analyses attribute the sweep
    to COLDCARD seeds that could be guessed. Everything dated before 30 July is
    the archaeology of how a build-time guard written in 2021 became a live
    key-generation defect.
    <Claim
      basis="verified"
      scope="The dated source transitions and vendor-published firmware and signed-release records in this timeline"
      source="Release histories, source commits and signed-release records; published binary contents were not independently checked"
      href="/record/firmware/"
      compact
    />
    The rungs below reconstruct 30 July to 3 August, over
    which two day-one publications disagreed about which models were affected and
    the vendor's own advisory was revised four times, and then the weekend in
    which the vendor described halting shipments and destroying stock, a board
    departure was announced, and a claimed fourth sweep wave was circulated and
    twice corrected.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      contested
      scope="The conflicting day-one affected-model statements from Coinkite and Block"
      source="Captured day-one publications from Coinkite and Block"
      href="#day-one-scope"
      compact
    />
    <Claim
      basis="verified"
      scope="The wording of both 1 August vendor advisory and backgrounder updates, the changed site-wide banner, and the later substitution of Mk2 and Mk3 for Mk3 throughout the advisory and backgrounder"
      source="Held captures of the Coinkite advisory, entropy backgrounder, downloads page and terms page taken at 14:02 and 18:44 UTC on 1 August 2026, with diffs against the preceding held states"
      href="#published-updates"
      compact
    />
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The 2 and 3 August entries: the vendor's shipments and outreach statements, the OpenSats board change, the reported passphrase-wallet loss, the claimed fourth wave with its corrections and Galaxy's confirmed-total revision"
      source="Captured X posts from Coinkite, OpenSats, Galaxy Research and named community accounts, each cited on its own entry"
      href="#august-2-3"
      compact
    />
    The swept totals, each kept with its source, its set and its date, are on
    <a href="/record/funds/">the funds-accounting page</a>.
  </p>
  </Answer>

  <div class="ported" set:html={spine} />

  <Disclosure
    id="sweeps-30-july"
    title="30 July: the sweeps, before anything was published"
    note="one 41-minute window, and the 500-transaction subset inside it"
    open
  >

  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-item is-bad">
      <div class="tl-date">30 Jul 2026, 01:10:20 to 01:51:26 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Galaxy later reported a 41-minute window covering 1,196 counted addresses
        and {fig("galaxyGrossBtc")}, beginning in block 960,183.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" contested scope="Galaxy's 41-minute wider window, address count and BTC estimate" source="Galaxy Research primary X post, preserved in the archive" href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-flow-map/" />
        Within it, the 500-transaction set reported by TFTC and Rob Hamilton
        spans about 15 minutes: a reported 1,324 UTXOs spent from 500 source
        addresses, with TFTC publishing <strong>{fig("sweptBtc")}</strong>
        received by one first collector and Hamilton reporting approximately
        594.48 BTC moved and about 562 BTC consolidated.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The 500-transaction set's reported UTXO count, duration, collector receipt and consolidation figure" source="Captured TFTC report and Rob Hamilton primary post" href="/record/sources/tftc-who-must-move/" />
        Bitcoin Optech states 29 July without a timezone; a US-local
        date is possible but not established in the newsletter. See
        <a href="/record/funds/">funds accounting</a> for the difference between
        the published totals and the sets they count.
        <CaptureThumb id="glxyresearch-flow-map" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="day-one-scope"
    title="30 July: the two day-one publications disagreed"
    note="Coinkite scoped the issue to Mk3; Block published a wider range the same day"
  >

  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">30 Jul 2026, Coinkite</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/">Coinkite's Mk3 Security Advisory</a>
        (<a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/coldcard-mk3-seed-generation-warning/" rel="noopener nofollow">original</a>)
        scoped the issue to Mk3 and said Mk4, Q and Mk5 were "not affected based
        on our early analysis". That earlier state is recovered from the Internet
        Archive; this project's own capture of the page began on 1 August.
        <Claim basis="verified" contested scope="The affected-model statement in Coinkite's first advisory" source="Coinkite Mk3 Security Advisory; original state recovered through the Internet Archive" href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">30 Jul 2026, Block</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="/record/sources/block-disclosure/">Block's engineering report</a>
        (<a href="https://engineering.block.xyz/blog/predictable-rng-fallback-and-32-bit-reseed-in-coldcard-firmware" rel="noopener nofollow">original</a>)
        published a wider affected-firmware range and described active
        exploitation. Block states that it published before the vendor's analysis
        was complete because it believed exploitation was active. The two day-one
        publications therefore gave different answers about Mk4-class devices.
        <Claim basis="reported" contested scope="Block's published affected-firmware range and exploitation account" source="Block engineering report, published 30 Jul 2026; the firmware range is separately checked against source" href="/record/sources/block-disclosure/" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ve-card ve-card--amber">
    <div class="ve-card__label lbl-amber">Interpretation limit</div>
    <p>
      The record establishes the disagreement and the wording of each statement.
      It does not establish why the analyses differed or what any individual
      owner read before acting.
    </p>
  </div>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="july-31"
    title="31 July: corrections, releases and wider accounting"
    note="the earlier-wave scan, the hotfixes, the wider attribution and the attribution finding"
  >

  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 00:03:31 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="/record/sources/clay-earlier-waves-thread/">Clay Garrett published Block's preliminary earlier-wave scan</a>,
        reporting <strong>{fig("earlierTxs")} additional transactions</strong>
        with the same stated fingerprint as the known wave: 204 sweeps in block
        960,183 and 491 in block 960,185, netting
        <strong>{fig("earlierBtc")}</strong> to three first collectors. The
        thread describes a scan of 888,661 transactions. A later privacy-safe
        recheck places those blocks inside Galaxy's 41-minute window and before
        the 500-transaction subset. Block explicitly said it had not confirmed a
        relationship between those transactions and the drain, so the
        common-operator attribution remains unverified.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="Block's preliminary 695-transaction fingerprint match, counts and net first-collector amount, including its unconfirmed relationship to the drain" source="Clay Garrett and Block engineering, captured primary X thread, 31 Jul 2026" href="/record/sources/clay-earlier-waves-thread/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="clay-earlier-waves-thread" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-good">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 03:44:44 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="https://x.com/unchained/status/2083036112449163618" rel="noopener nofollow">Unchained recommended rotating any COLDCARD-generated key</a>
        to one generated on a different device. It identified two affected keys
        in a 2-of-3 vault as the most severe case and said one affected key is
        normally below threshold, while still recommending replacement.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" scope="Unchained's public client guidance" source="Unchained, preserved X capture" href="/record/sources/unchained-guidance/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="unchained-guidance" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-good">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 05:17 to 05:19 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        The signed-release record timestamps Q hotfix v1.5.0Q at 05:17 UTC and
        Mk4/Mk5 hotfix v5.6.0 at 05:18 UTC. Source release commit
        <code>3238f6fd9977eed786012d0034a04d888c3263bb</code> followed at
        05:19:10 UTC with the note: "No new features but will generate entropy
        correctly." The wording is about what the device will generate next; it
        does not repair already generated seeds. This project verified the source
        and release records, not the contents of the distributed binaries.
        <Claim basis="verified" scope="The vendor-published Mk4/Mk5/Q release-record and source-commit timestamps, not binary contents" source="COLDCARD firmware repository and signed release metadata" href="/record/firmware/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">30 Jul 2026, publisher date; expanded-scope time unresolved</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Coinkite's <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-backgrounder/">entropy technical backgrounder</a>
        (<a href="https://blog.coinkite.com/entropy-technical-backgrounder/" rel="noopener nofollow">original</a>)
        displays a 30 July publication date. In the state held on 1 August it
        includes Mk4, Mk5 and Q and presents scenarios near 72 bits for those
        models and 40 bits for Mk3. The first appearance of that expanded scope
        has not been established, so its ordering relative to the 31 July
        05:19 UTC firmware commit remains unresolved. The entry is placed
        beside that commit rather than in date order, because there is no
        established time to sort it by.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" contested scope="Coinkite's expanded affected scope and published candidate-space models; not the first-publication time" source="Coinkite entropy technical backgrounder; source-level affected ranges are checked separately" href="/record/sources/coinkite-backgrounder/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-good">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 11:37 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        An <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-expanded-scope/">official COLDCARD update</a>
        publicly directs users of affected Mk3, Mk4, Mk5 and Q firmware toward
        model-specific migration: Mk3 users on v4.0.1 or later without at least
        50 private independent dice rolls should migrate, and affected Mk4, Mk5
        and Q users should update before generating a new seed. Its Mk3 wording
        starts at v4.0.1, while the source and release evidence includes v4.0.0.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" contested scope="The vendor's expanded public scope and migration direction, including its v4.0.1 Mk3 boundary" source="Official COLDCARD X update; the v4.0.0 source-evidence disagreement is documented on the firmware page" href="/record/sources/coldcard-expanded-scope/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-expanded-scope" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 13:23:11 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="https://x.com/glxyresearch/status/2083181683067506899" rel="noopener nofollow">Galaxy Research published the wider {fig("galaxyGrossBtc")} estimate</a>
        and attributed 1,196 counted addresses in blocks 960,183 to 960,191 to
        the same operator. The exact Galaxy address-counting method and the
        underlying transaction list are not published.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" scope="Galaxy's wider-set attribution and estimate" source="Galaxy Research primary X post, preserved X capture" href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-flow-map/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-good">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 13:43:05 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        The <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-mk3-hotfix-update/">official Mk3 update</a>
        announces firmware <strong>4.2.0</strong> as available. The 07:30 UTC
        advisory state had described a final Mk3 release as under evaluation
        rather than committed, contingent on validating a sufficiently safe
        upgrade path, and told users not to wait for it. The vendor downloads
        page checked on 1 August lists 4.2.0 as current for "COLDCARD Mk3 and
        Mk2". This archive has not installed the release on either model or
        independently checked the binary.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The vendor's availability announcement for Mk3 4.2.0 and downloads-page listing for Mk2 and Mk3" source="Captured official COLDCARD X update and vendor downloads page; source-level fix and binary limitations are documented separately" href="/record/firmware/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-mk3-hotfix-update" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 15:42:23 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="https://x.com/nvk/status/2083216713693151552" rel="noopener nofollow">NVK published an apology on behalf of Coinkite</a>,
        accepted responsibility for the firmware bug, committed to a technical
        postmortem covering why their own review missed it, and offered written
        incident summaries and available transaction data to affected users for
        police reports and insurance claims.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" scope="NVK's apology and stated commitments" source="NVK, preserved X capture" href="/record/sources/nvk-apology/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-good">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 16:54:38 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        The <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-edge-hotfix-update/">official Edge update</a>
        announces 6.6.0X for Mk4/Mk5 and 6.6.0QX for Q. Mk3, Mk4, Mk5 and Q then
        have named vendor fix releases. The binaries have not been independently
        checked here.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The official availability announcement for Edge 6.6.0X and 6.6.0QX" source="Captured official COLDCARD X update; binary limitations are documented separately" href="/record/firmware/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-edge-hotfix-update" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 17:42:45 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="https://x.com/clay_garrett/status/2083247006139503065" rel="noopener nofollow">Block's Clay Garrett reported</a>
        that the operator used <strong>a paid account at a well-known
        blockchain-services provider</strong> to query the source addresses
        during the sweeps, and that the provider's internal logs matched the
        suspected workflow "with extraordinary specificity, including the number,
        timing and sequence of requests". Shared with authorities. He said Block
        had seen no evidence the provider knowingly participated in or
        facilitated the suspected theft.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" scope="Block's account of the service-provider queries and its non-participation qualification" source="Clay Garrett, complete three-post X thread preserved in the archive" href="/record/sources/clay-attribution/" />
        <Claim
          basis="reported"
          scope="The later operator attribution and public accounts of how the bug may have been discovered"
          source="Captured statements from Block, Coinkite and named researchers"
          href="#july-31"
          compact
        />
        <CaptureThumb id="clay-attribution" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">31 Jul 2026, 21:18:10 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="https://x.com/KLoaec/status/2083301216050700780" rel="noopener nofollow">Kevin Loaec published configuration-specific guidance</a>
        for multisig and miniscript, including the condition where affected
        COLDCARD keys meet the spending threshold. The post described Taproot and
        private transaction submission as potentially relevant in some cases,
        not as universal guarantees.
        <Claim basis="reported" captured="1 Aug 2026" scope="Loaec's dated multisig and miniscript guidance" source="Kevin Loaec, preserved X capture" href="/record/sources/kloaec-multisig/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="kloaec-multisig" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="august-1"
    title="1 August: trackers, third-party reports and two advisory revisions"
    note="the advisory's third and fourth dated updates, and Galaxy's third wave"
  >

  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, captured 05:28:59 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        The community <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-watch/">coldcard-watch tracker</a>
        expands its headline from the first 1,195-address episode to two episodes
        totalling 2,321 addresses and 1,128.4717 BTC. This records the tracker's
        changed attribution. The relationship of the later 45.9 BTC cluster to
        the principal incident set remains unverified.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 11:21 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Coinkite <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-urgent-migration-appeal/">ask owners to treat migration as urgent</a>:
        follow the advisory for the model, upgrade the device, generate a new
        seed, move funds carefully, and pass the message to owners who are less
        online. The post quotes the vendor's 31 July urgent update, which states
        the Mk3 boundary as 4.0.1 and later and carves out seeds created with at
        least 50 private, independent dice rolls.
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-urgent-migration-appeal" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 12:29 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Kevin Loaec of Wizardsardine <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-mk4-class-drain-report/">reports</a>
        that Mk4, Mk5 and Q wallets are "now actively drained", quoting Tomer
        Strolight's account of a duress wallet, deliberately left funded on a
        COLDCARD Mk4 seed and swept overnight to
        <code>bc1qzm5pauxyv7t7vqstzpumqcn066wfjsmev34mf3</code>. The same post
        argues that breaking an Mk4 is harder than breaking a weak passphrase, so
        Mk3 seeds behind a passphrase at or under roughly 32 bits are at
        immediate risk. This is a reported third-party account of a single
        wallet. The pinned funds dataset here covers the three 30 July waves and
        holds no addresses, so it cannot corroborate the sweep. The named
        destination does appear in held captures of a community tracker as a
        1 August vault holding 0.33203236 BTC from 16 sweeps, and a later capture
        of that tracker records its Mk4 attribution as withdrawn in favour of an
        Mk3-origin seed. The device model behind a sweep is not visible on chain.
        <Claim
          basis="reported"
          contested
          scope="The 1 August account that Mk4, Mk5 and Q wallets were being actively drained, and the honeypot sweep quoted with it"
          source="Kevin Loaec's captured post quoting Tomer Strolight; a community tracker's later held capture records the Mk4 attribution as withdrawn in favour of an Mk3-origin seed"
          href="/record/sources/kloaec-mk4-class-drain-report/"
          compact
        />
        <CaptureThumb id="kloaec-mk4-class-drain-report" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 09:35 EDT (13:35 UTC); captured 14:02:34 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/">Coinkite's Mk3 Security Advisory</a>
        carries a third dated update. It replaces the earlier blanket warning
        that Mk3 4.0.1 to 4.1.9 users' funds "may be at risk" with a conditional
        statement: funds are at risk "if the seed was created without at least 50
        fair, independent, private dice rolls and the funded wallet is not
        protected by a strong, unique BIP-39 passphrase". The passphrase section
        moved in both directions in the same edit. It now says the reduced seed
        entropy alone is not enough to reach funds in a passphrase wallet and
        that "an attacker must also discover the passphrase", and it also says a
        strong passphrase "does not repair the affected seed", that passphrase
        users "should also migrate as soon as practical", and that an uncertain
        passphrase means treating the funds as at risk and migrating
        immediately. The
        <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-backgrounder/">entropy backgrounder</a>
        carries the same dated update, a paragraph setting out what counts as a
        qualifying passphrase, and a new sentence that for funded wallets with
        neither the dice entropy nor such a passphrase "the reduced search space
        is a direct security risk, not a theoretical possibility". In the same
        period the site-wide banner on the
        <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-downloads/">downloads</a> and
        <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-terms/">terms</a> pages changed from
        "Seeds generated on firmware 4.0.1 or later may be at risk" to "Seeds
        generated on firmware 4.0.1 (2021 or later) are at risk".
        <Claim basis="verified" scope="The wording of the 1 August advisory and backgrounder update and of the changed site-wide banner" source="Held captures of the Coinkite Mk3 advisory, entropy backgrounder, downloads page and terms page, with diffs against the preceding held states" href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 15:45 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Wizardsardine publish a <a href="/record/sources/wizardsardine-postmortem/">long-form post-mortem</a>,
        announced by <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-wizardsardine-postmortem/">Kevin Loaec</a>.
        It addresses Liana and multisig configurations first, then reconstructs
        the flaw, and argues the incident is worse than commonly understood
        because owners with dice-generated seeds still have device features that
        depend on the same generator. Wizardsardine sells Liana, a competing
        wallet product, and the authors describe the post as written quickly and
        invite corrections.
        <CaptureThumb id="kloaec-wizardsardine-postmortem" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 15:47 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Loaec separately <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-derived-feature-exposure/">states</a>
        that owners who imported a seed or generated one with dice are also
        exposed if they used certain COLDCARD features. The features are
        enumerated in an attached graphic that did not render in the held
        capture, so the list itself is not established from what is held here.
        The claim extends the incident beyond seed generation into derived
        material; <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/">what else the generator touched</a>
        tracks which features are established from source.
        <CaptureThumb id="kloaec-derived-feature-exposure" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 16:09 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        A separate matter involving the same party: Block's Clay Garrett
        <a href="/record/sources/claygarrett-bitkey-initial-findings/">publishes initial findings</a>
        on a reported vulnerability in Block's own Bitkey product, disclosed to
        Block by 1440000bytes. This is not the COLDCARD RNG defect and does not
        bear on it. Block states that exploitation would require exceptional
        circumstances during a narrow window in inheritance setup, that an
        attacker would not obtain enough cryptographic material to reach funds,
        that it sees no risk of remote drains, and that a mobile-app patch would
        be submitted the same day.
        <CaptureThumb id="claygarrett-bitkey-initial-findings" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 16:35 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        bitcoin++ Insider Edition <a href="/record/sources/btcpp-dettmer-analysis-announcement/">announce</a>
        Dustin Dettmer's <a href="/record/sources/btcpp-dettmer-commit-history/">commit-history walkthrough</a>,
        "When random.bytes() runs but doesn't work". It reads the firmware
        history around the disabled hardware-RNG guard and the call chain that
        left seed generation on the software generator.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 14:35 EDT (18:35 UTC); captured 18:44:33 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        A fourth dated update brings the Mk2 inside the advisory's own text. Every
        Mk3-only statement about the defect and about its fix is rewritten to
        name both models. The affected range becomes "The issue is present on
        Mk2 and Mk3 firmware versions 4.0.1 through 4.1.9 inclusive"; the at-risk
        sentence becomes "a seed generated on Mk2 or Mk3 version 4.0.1 (March
        2021) through 4.1.9"; the fixed-firmware list gives "Mk2/Mk3: version
        4.2.0 or later"; and the release is described as "Fixed Mk2/Mk3 firmware
        version 4.2.0", to be installed from the "official Mk2/Mk3 download
        page". The one-device migration section, the optional dice-only section
        and the closing migration steps are rewritten the same way, so the
        procedures the advisory sets out now address an Mk2 owner directly. The
        <a href="/record/sources/coinkite-backgrounder/">entropy backgrounder</a>
        carries the same dated update and the same substitution, including that
        the active PRNG on "Mk2 and Mk3" was seeded primarily from device and
        timing state, and "Version 4.2.0 for Mk2 and Mk3" among the released
        hotfixes. Until this revision the vendor downloads-page listing was the
        only vendor evidence placing the Mk2 in either the affected range or the
        hotfix. The published lower bound is unchanged at 4.0.1 for both models,
        so the <a href="/record/firmware/">v4.0.0 boundary difference</a> stands.
        <Claim basis="verified" scope="The fourth revision's substitution of Mk2 and Mk3 for Mk3 throughout the affected range, the fixed release and the migration sections of both the advisory and the backgrounder" source="Held captures of the Coinkite Mk3 advisory and entropy technical backgrounder taken at 18:44 UTC on 1 August 2026, with diffs against the states held at 14:02 UTC the same day" href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">1 Aug 2026, 18:38 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Galaxy Research <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-third-wave-revision/">identify a third sweep wave</a>
        of <strong>{fig("thirdWaveBtc")}</strong> and revise their estimated
        observed size of the incident to
        <strong>{fig("galaxyRevisedTotalBtc")}</strong> across
        {fig("galaxyRevisedAddresses")}, superseding the roughly 1,083 BTC total
        they published on 31 July. The same thread reports
        <strong>{fig("attackerHoldingsBtc")}</strong> under attacker control with
        all endpoint attacker addresses fully unspent on chain, and carries
        Galaxy's <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-methodology-disclaimer/">own method limit</a>:
        the work derives solely from block data and the unspent-output set, and
        Galaxy has not tested whether the addresses it identifies as possible
        victims were in fact generated with low entropy. Galaxy also
        <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-firmware-block-boundary/">states</a>
        that the vulnerable firmware shipped on 17 March 2021 around block
        674,951 and that no coin identified in waves 1 to 3 was created before
        that block; what that means for the published version ranges is on
        <a href="/record/firmware/">the firmware page</a>. Galaxy's scope is wider
        than the 30 July set its 31 July figure covered;
        <a href="/record/funds/">the funds-accounting page</a> keeps both as
        dated figures.
        <Claim
          basis="reported"
          scope="Galaxy's 1 August third wave, revised total, attacker-holdings figure and stated method limit, and its statement about the firmware ship block"
          source="Galaxy Research's captured 1 August thread; its scope is wider than the 30 July set and the underlying transaction and address lists are not published"
          href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-third-wave-revision/"
          compact
        />
        <CaptureThumb id="glxyresearch-third-wave-revision" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Four dated advisory revisions are recorded here: the widening of scope to
    Mk4, Mk5 and Q, the announcement of fixed firmware, the replacement of a
    blanket warning with two published conditions, and the addition of the Mk2.
    None of them moves the published lower bound, which stays at 4.0.1 and so
    leaves the <a href="/record/firmware/#vendor-lower-bound">difference with the
    v4.0.0 source and release evidence</a> as it was.
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="august-2-3"
    title="2 to 3 August: vendor operations, a board departure and a claimed fourth wave"
    note="the shipments statement, the Sunday update, the OpenSats departure and the twice-corrected wave-4 set"
  >

  <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 17:24 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Galaxy Research <a href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-victim-outreach-update/">publish an investigation status update</a>:
        the wave-1 pattern came from Block engineers, while waves 2 and 3 were
        identified through victims coming forward. The update asks victims to
        share affected addresses and transaction IDs with @intangiblecoins, with
        no personal identifying information needed, says findings are being
        shared with US authorities, SEAL, exchanges and cyber investigators, and
        promises a substantive update.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="Galaxy's account of how each wave was identified, its victim-outreach request and its statement about sharing findings with investigators" source="Galaxy Research, captured X update, 2 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/glxyresearch-victim-outreach-update/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="glxyresearch-victim-outreach-update" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 18:04 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Coinkite <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/">state that shipments were halted</a>
        as soon as the vulnerability was confirmed, and that remaining units
        carrying affected firmware were destroyed. Customers whose devices had
        already shipped were emailed the advisory and migration steps. The same
        post states that SATSCARD, OPENDIME and TAPSIGNER use different
        codebases and are not affected.
        <a href="/record/sources/tftc-shipments-halted/">TFTC repeated the same facts</a>
        just under two and a half hours later; the vendor post is the primary
        statement.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The vendor's shipments-halted and inventory-destruction statement, its customer-email account and its not-affected statement for SATSCARD, OPENDIME and TAPSIGNER" source="Coinkite, captured official X post, 2 Aug 2026; TFTC's later secondary report is held separately" href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-shipments-halted" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 19:51 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Replying to a question about the disclaimer added to its store, Coinkite
        <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-disclaimer-clarification/">say the notice exists</a>
        so previous buyers are aware of the incident. An earlier
        <a href="/record/sources/pledditor-remaining-stock/">community claim</a>
        that remaining stock stayed on sale with an added disclosure, rather than
        sales being halted, is held separately.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The vendor's stated purpose of the store disclaimer" source="Coinkite, captured official X reply, 2 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/coldcard-disclaimer-clarification/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-disclaimer-clarification" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 20:44 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        OpenSats <a href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/">announce that NVK is stepping down</a>
        from its board, effective immediately, with the board continuing at
        eight members until a replacement is made. The post gives no reason for
        the departure; its timing places it inside the incident weekend, and the
        post itself does not connect the two.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The OpenSats board departure and its stated interim board size; the post states no reason" source="OpenSats, captured X statement, 2 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="opensats-nvk-board-departure" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 21:13 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        BTC Sessions <a href="/record/sources/btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report/">reports what he describes as the first confirmed loss</a>
        from a Mk3 protected by a passphrase: a two-word passphrase, with the
        wallet drained at around 2pm on 2 August in Australian eastern time
        (AEST, UTC+10), which is about 04:00 UTC the same day. The post cites no transaction or address, so
        nothing held here can corroborate or refute it. If accurate, it would
        extend the observed drains to weak-passphrase wallets; what a passphrase
        does and does not protect is set out on
        <a href="/risk/">the risk pages</a>.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="BTC Sessions' account of a drained Mk3 two-word-passphrase wallet and its claimed timing; the post cites no transaction or address and the loss is not corroborated here" source="BTC Sessions, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">2 Aug 2026, 23:00 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Coinkite publish an <a href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/">"Update, Sunday" statement</a>
        acknowledging that the damage done is permanent and that there are hard
        questions about the company, describing direct customer outreach and
        migration help since Friday, thanking community members who helped
        unpaid through the weekend, and committing to continued work with the
        wider self-custody community.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The vendor's Sunday statement: its damage acknowledgement, outreach description, community thanks and forward commitment" source="Coinkite, captured official X post, 2 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-update-sunday" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">3 Aug 2026, 01:37 and 03:22 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        A claimed fourth sweep wave, identified by pattern matching and
        circulated by Galaxy's intangiblecoins, is corrected twice in public.
        The <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-address-correction/">first correction</a>
        removes six of 216 destination addresses whose years of history before
        block 960,183 made them unlikely attacker addresses, leaving 206 of the
        remainder freshly created to receive the sweeps.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The removal of six historical destination addresses from the circulated wave-4 list and the 206 freshly created remainder" source="intangiblecoins, captured correction post, 3 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-address-correction/" />
        The
        <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/">second correction</a>,
        credited to Nunchuk, removes multisig addresses included in error: the
        post states wave 4 as circulated at 857 addresses and 486.11 BTC, an
        89-address, 20.58 BTC multisig discount, and a surviving core of 709
        addresses and 448.73 BTC, all as the author's own figures. It also
        states that waves 1 to 3 contain no multisig addresses, and stresses
        that wave 4 rests on pattern matching with no direct victim
        confirmation yet.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="The multisig correction, the stated wave-4 figures as circulated and as corrected, and the author's statement that wave 4 has no direct victim confirmation" source="intangiblecoins, captured correction post credited to Nunchuk, 3 Aug 2026" href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/" />
        The wave-4 set remains unconfirmed and is not adopted
        by <a href="/record/funds/">the funds-accounting page</a>.
        <CaptureThumb id="intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item is-key">
      <div class="tl-date">3 Aug 2026, 22:51 UTC</div>
      <div class="tl-body">
        Galaxy Research <a href="/record/sources/glxy-losses-exceed-100m/">revise their top line again</a>:
        high-confidence <strong>{fig("galaxyConfirmedBtc")}</strong> stolen from
        {fig("galaxyConfirmedAddresses")} across three confirmed waves plus 14
        smaller incidents, stated as exceeding US$100M and superseding the
        1 August {fig("galaxyRevisedTotalBtc")} figure. Wave 1 is now fully
        confirmed through victim reports, 73 individual victims have contacted
        @intangiblecoins, and the 14 smaller footprints
        <a href="/record/sources/glxy-victim-count/">could be many different attackers</a>
        individually exploiting the now-known vulnerability. Including a
        suspected fourth wave would take the total to
        <strong>{fig("galaxyInclWave4Btc")}</strong>, but Galaxy
        <a href="/record/sources/glxy-wave4-caveat/">does not promote it</a>
        without victim confirmation. Galaxy states that 90 percent of stolen
        coins have not moved and 100 percent of waves 1 to 3 coins are unmoved,
        that confirmed attacker and victim addresses have been
        <a href="/record/sources/glxy-law-enforcement-handoff/">provided to US federal law enforcement, exchanges and compliance firms</a>,
        and that <a href="/record/sources/glxy-attack-ongoing/">the attack is ongoing</a>.
        <Claim basis="reported" scope="Galaxy's 3 August confirmed and wave-4-inclusive totals, confirmation basis, victim count, unmoved-coins statements, law-enforcement handoff and ongoing-attack warning" source="Galaxy Research's captured 3 August thread; the underlying transaction and address lists are not published" href="/record/sources/glxy-losses-exceed-100m/" />
        <CaptureThumb id="glxy-losses-exceed-100m" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="published-updates"
    title="Published updates, side by side"
    note="each subject's earlier and later published wording"
  >

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <caption>Changes in published incident scope
          <Claim basis="verified" scope="The earlier and later published wordings compared in the table above" source="Held captures of the Coinkite Mk3 advisory at 01:56 and 07:30 UTC on 31 July 2026, both recovered from the Internet Archive, plus the captured backgrounder, the 13:43 UTC official Mk3 update, and this project's own captures of the advisory, downloads page and terms page on 1 August 2026, including the advisory states held at 14:02 and 18:44 UTC that bound the Mk2-coverage row" href="/record/sources/coinkite-mk3-advisory/" />
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col" class="nw">Subject</th><th scope="col" class="wide">Earlier publication</th><th scope="col" class="wide">Later publication</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Affected models</td>
            <td class="wide">Coinkite's 30 July advisory said Mk4, Q and Mk5 were not affected based on early analysis.</td>
            <td class="wide">The backgrounder's state held on 1 August included Mk4, Mk5 and Q in the affected scope. The page displays a 30 July publication date; the expanded scope's first appearance time is unresolved.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Mk2 coverage</td>
            <td class="wide">Through the state captured at 14:02 UTC on 1 August, which is the first held after the 13:35 UTC update, the advisory named only one model: "The issue is present on Mk3 firmware versions 4.0.1 through 4.1.9 inclusive", with the fixed release given as "Mk3: version 4.2.0 or later".</td>
            <td class="wide">The 14:35 EDT update of 1 August names both: "The issue is present on Mk2 and Mk3 firmware versions 4.0.1 through 4.1.9 inclusive", with the fixed release given as "Mk2/Mk3: version 4.2.0 or later". The lower bound of 4.0.1 is unchanged.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Mk3 firmware</td>
            <td class="wide">The 31 July advisory said Coinkite was exploring whether it could safely publish one final Mk3 release, contingent on validating a sufficiently safe upgrade path, and warned users not to wait for it.</td>
            <td class="wide">Firmware 4.2.0 was published for Mk3 at 13:43 UTC on 31 July.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Who is at risk</td>
            <td class="wide">Through the state held at 00:17 UTC on 1 August, the advisory said Coinkite was "warning all users who generated a seed using a Mk3 on version 4.0.1 (March 2021) thru 4.1.9 (inclusive) that their funds may be at risk".</td>
            <td class="wide">The 09:35 EDT update of 1 August states that funds are at risk "if the seed was created without at least 50 fair, independent, private dice rolls and the funded wallet is not protected by a strong, unique BIP-39 passphrase". The blanket warning becomes two published conditions.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Passphrase guidance</td>
            <td class="wide">The earlier text said a strong, unique BIP-39 passphrase "adds an independent barrier" and that "the risk depends on the strength of the passphrase", with short, common, patterned, quoted or reused passphrases not to be assumed low risk.</td>
            <td class="wide">The same section now says the reduced seed entropy alone is not enough to reach funds in that wallet because "an attacker must also discover the passphrase", and separately that a strong passphrase "does not repair the affected seed", that passphrase users "should also migrate as soon as practical", and that an uncertain passphrase means treating the funds as at risk and migrating immediately.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Site-wide banner</td>
            <td class="wide">The banner carried on the downloads and terms pages read "Seeds generated on firmware 4.0.1 or later may be at risk".</td>
            <td class="wide">The same banner reads "Seeds generated on firmware 4.0.1 (2021 or later) are at risk".</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nw">Attributed transaction accounting</td>
            <td class="wide">Early public figures described the 500-transaction, approximately 594.5 BTC set.</td>
            <td class="wide">Galaxy later published a wider {fig("galaxyGrossBtc")} attribution including 695 additional transactions.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="unresolved"
    title="Unresolved as of 3 August 2026"
    note="eight questions the held record does not settle"
  >

  <ul class="node-list">
    <li><strong>The reported Mk4-class sweep.</strong> Kevin Loaec of Wizardsardine <a href="/record/sources/kloaec-mk4-class-drain-report/">reported on 1 August</a> that Mk4, Mk5 and Q wallets were being actively drained, quoting a third-party account of a deliberately funded honeypot wallet swept overnight. Nothing held here settles which device model generated its seed.</li>
    <li><strong>Backgrounder ordering.</strong> The page displays a 30 July publication date, but the first appearance of its expanded model scope relative to the 31 July 05:19 UTC hotfix commit remains unknown.</li>
    <li><strong>The wider transaction attribution.</strong> Block's captured preliminary thread and the privacy-safe recheck support the 695-transaction counts and arithmetic, but no complete primary transaction list is published here and the arithmetic does not establish common control.</li>
    <li><strong>Later clusters.</strong> In their states checked on 1 August, two community trackers included a separately reported 45.9 BTC cluster in wider headlines. Its relationship to the principal incident set remains an attributed lead rather than a verified extension of the total.</li>
    <li><strong>Original discovery method.</strong> AI-assisted discovery remains an inference; AI-assisted post-disclosure reproduction is separately reported.</li>
    <li><strong>Number of affected people and seeds.</strong> Addresses are not people, and no captured source supplies the total number of seeds generated on affected firmware.</li>
    <li><strong>The claimed fourth wave.</strong> The pattern-matched wave-4 set circulated on 2 and 3 August was <a href="/record/sources/intangiblecoins-wave4-multisig-correction/">corrected twice by its own compiler</a>, who states there is no direct victim confirmation yet. Whether the surviving core belongs to the incident at all is not settled by anything held here.</li>
    <li><strong>The reported passphrase-wallet loss.</strong> BTC Sessions' <a href="/record/sources/btcsessions-passphrase-loss-report/">2 August report</a> of a drained Mk3 with a two-word passphrase cites no transaction or address, so it can be neither corroborated nor refuted from the held record.</li>
  </ul>

  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="how-to-check"
    title="How to check this reconstruction"
    note="what is held, and what is inherited"
  >

  <p>
    Each source page shows what we hold: the excerpted text, the hashes and the
    diffs between one check and the next. Social posts link to the original.
    Anything recovered from the Internet Archive rather than captured here is
    labelled as such, so an inherited copy is never presented as one this project
    took at the time.
  </p>

  </Disclosure>

  <p>
    Predictable key generation has appeared before, in Debian OpenSSL, Android
    SecureRandom, Randstorm, Milk Sad and Trust Wallet; the
    <a href="/response/#precedent">precedent comparison</a> sets them beside their
    differences stated, and the <a href="/response/ai/">AI evidence page</a>
    separates Coinkite's inference about how the defect was found from the
    separately reported post-disclosure reproductions. One limit belongs with
    both: a successful reproducible-build comparison can show that a distributed
    binary corresponds to specified source and build inputs, but not that the
    source logic is correct.
  </p>
